<?xml version="1.0"?>
<info xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	  xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://apps.nextcloud.com/schema/apps/info.xsd">
	<!--
		THE APP ID IS `thematiq`. THE DESIGN SYSTEM IS `nldesign`. THEY ARE NOT
		THE SAME NAME AND ONLY ONE OF THEM MOVED.

		This element is what Nextcloud namespaces `oc_appconfig`, `oc_preferences`,
		`/apps/<id>/` routes, `script()`/`style()`/`loadState()` channels and the
		l10n domain by. It changed from `nldesign` to `thematiq` on 2026-08-22.

		"NL Design System" is an external standard owned by the Dutch government.
		Its names — the CSS custom properties prefixed `nldesign`, the `nldesign`
		class/DOM-id prefixes, the `nldesign` design-system id in
		design-systems.json, nldesignsystem.nl, the nl-design-system/themes
		upstream — did NOT change and MUST NOT be changed here. They are matched
		by string against stylesheets in every other app in the fleet and in
		@conduction/nextcloud-vue; renaming one here does not rename it there,
		so the selector simply stops matching and the theme silently falls back
		to default styling with nothing in any log.
	-->
	<id>thematiq</id>
	<name lang="en">Thematiq</name>
	<name lang="nl">Thematiq</name>
	<summary lang="en">Your house style on every Nextcloud screen. Edit it live, no code needed.</summary>
	<summary lang="nl">Je huisstijl op elk Nextcloud-scherm. Live aanpassen, zonder code.</summary>
	<description lang="en"><![CDATA[Style your whole Nextcloud from one settings page. No CSS, no developer, no rebuild.

Nextcloud's own Theming sets a colour, a background and a logo. Thematiq styles everything else: buttons, tables, status colours, fonts and corner radius. You see each change live before you save it. After saving, every app follows. That includes Files, Talk and Calendar, and Conduction apps such as OpenRegister and OpenCatalogi.

**What you can do**

- **Edit live.** Four tabs cover the login page, content, buttons and typography. Reset any value with one click.
- **Use your own fonts.** Upload the web fonts your organisation has a licence for. They are served from your own server.
- **Save your own themes.** Keep a design as a reusable theme. Or import a published design-system theme and see exactly what was applied.
- **Start from a ready-made theme.** Stock Nextcloud, La Suite numérique, or the house style of a Dutch government organisation. The government themes follow NL Design System.
- **Try it before your users do.** Preview a theme in your own session. Nobody else sees it until you apply it.
- **Set a theme per group.** Give a department its own look. Exclude apps that should keep the default.

**What your IT team gets**

- **Your own branding.** Thematiq works on top of Nextcloud's own Theming. Your name, logo and background stay as you set them.
- **Contrast report.** Check the active theme against the WCAG contrast rules, the web accessibility standard. Use the result in your accessibility statement.
- **Branded e-mail.** Notification e-mails follow your theme. Add an optional footer.
- **Portable configuration.** Export the full setup as one file and import it on another server.
- **2,526 government icons.** From RVO, OpenGemeenten, Gemeente Den Haag and the French DSFR, for every app.
- **Free under EUPL-1.2.** No licence fee per user.

**About the organisation themes.** They copy the house style of the organisation they are named after. That organisation has not endorsed them. Use one only if your organisation may use that house style.

Create a [bug report](https://github.com/ConductionNL/thematiq/issues/new/choose)

Create a [feature request](https://github.com/ConductionNL/thematiq/issues/new/choose)

**Support:** For support, contact support@conduction.nl. For a Service Level Agreement (SLA), contact sales@conduction.nl.
	]]></description>
	<description lang="nl"><![CDATA[Geef je hele Nextcloud je eigen huisstijl, vanaf één instellingenpagina. Geen CSS, geen ontwikkelaar, geen nieuwe build.

De eigen Theming van Nextcloud regelt een kleur, een achtergrond en een logo. Thematiq doet de rest: knoppen, tabellen, statuskleuren, lettertypen en hoekafronding. Je ziet elke wijziging live voordat je opslaat. Na het opslaan volgt elke app. Dus ook Bestanden, Talk en Agenda, en Conduction-apps zoals OpenRegister en OpenCatalogi.

**Wat je kunt doen**

- **Live aanpassen.** Vier tabbladen voor de inlogpagina, inhoud, knoppen en typografie. Zet elke waarde met één klik terug.
- **Eigen lettertypen gebruiken.** Upload de webfonts waarvoor je organisatie een licentie heeft. Ze komen van je eigen server.
- **Eigen thema's bewaren.** Sla een ontwerp op als herbruikbaar thema. Of importeer een gepubliceerd design-system-thema en zie precies wat is toegepast.
- **Beginnen met een kant-en-klaar thema.** Standaard Nextcloud, La Suite numérique of de huisstijl van een Nederlandse overheidsorganisatie. De overheidsthema's volgen NL Design System.
- **Eerst zelf proberen.** Bekijk een thema in je eigen sessie. Niemand anders ziet het totdat je het toepast.
- **Een thema per groep instellen.** Geef een afdeling een eigen uitstraling. Sluit apps uit die de standaardweergave moeten houden.

**Wat je IT-team krijgt**

- **Je eigen branding.** Thematiq werkt bovenop de eigen Theming van Nextcloud. Je naam, logo en achtergrond blijven zoals je ze instelde.
- **Contrastrapport.** Toets het actieve thema aan de WCAG-contrastregels, de norm voor digitale toegankelijkheid. Gebruik de uitkomst in je toegankelijkheidsverklaring.
- **E-mail in je huisstijl.** Notificatiemails volgen je thema. Voeg desgewenst een footer toe.
- **Configuratie die meeverhuist.** Exporteer de volledige instellingen als één bestand en importeer ze op een andere server.
- **2.526 overheidsiconen.** Van RVO, OpenGemeenten, Gemeente Den Haag en het Franse DSFR, voor elke app.
- **Gratis onder EUPL-1.2.** Geen licentiekosten per gebruiker.

**Over de organisatiethema's.** Ze bootsen de huisstijl na van de organisatie waarnaar ze vernoemd zijn. Die organisatie heeft ze niet goedgekeurd. Gebruik er alleen een als je organisatie die huisstijl mag gebruiken.

Meld een [bug](https://github.com/ConductionNL/thematiq/issues/new/choose)

Doe een [featureverzoek](https://github.com/ConductionNL/thematiq/issues/new/choose)

**Ondersteuning:** Voor ondersteuning, neem contact op via support@conduction.nl. Voor een Service Level Agreement (SLA), neem contact op via sales@conduction.nl.
	]]></description>
	<!--
		1.1.0, not 0.1.3-unstable.17.

		This manifest under-reported the app by a whole major. The repo's
		newest stable tag is v1.0.0, the 1.1.0 line was already opened as
		v1.1.0-beta.0 and v1.1.0-beta.1, and apps.nextcloud.com currently
		serves 1.1.0-beta.1 — while this file still claimed 0.1.3.

		It survived because the release workflows rewrite the version element
		in the BUILT package rather than reading this value, so the number
		that ships has always been right and this one was never consulted.
		It is still the baseline the workflow falls back to when no stable
		tag exists, and it is what a reader checks to learn what this app is.

		NOTE FOR ANYONE EDITING THIS COMMENT: never write the opening
		version tag literally in this prose, not even inside quotes or an
		example command. The release workflow extracts the number with a
		lookbehind for that tag and takes the FIRST match in the whole file,
		so a literal occurrence up here outranks the real element below and
		the release builds itself a version out of comment text.

		This is not hypothetical. Drafting this very comment broke it twice:
		the first version made the workflow read " in the", and the second —
		which quoted the grep command in order to WARN about the problem —
		made it read ")[^". The warning reproduced the bug it described.
	-->
	<version>1.2.17-unstable.20261004084344</version>
	<licence>EUPL-1.2</licence>
	<author mail="info@conduction.nl" homepage="https://www.conduction.nl/">Conduction</author>
	<namespace>Thematiq</namespace>
	<category>customization</category>
	<!--
		GitHub, not Codeberg. The fleet dropped Forgejo/Codeberg on
		2026-08-19 and GitHub is the only forge again. Every URL below
		still answered 200 from a stale mirror, so nothing looked broken —
		but `bugs` pointed reporters at a tracker nobody reads, and the
		App Store fetches the screenshots itself.

		2026-08-22: repo renamed ConductionNL/nldesign -> ConductionNL/thematiq.
		The URLs below use the NEW name. GitHub also redirects the old name,
		so both work; the canonical one is written here.

		2026-09-29: the screenshots were replaced. The App Store downloads
		them from `main` when a release is uploaded, so each file under img/
		must be on `main` before that release goes out, or the listing shows
		a broken image. The store has no app-icon field: the tile in its app
		listings is the first screenshot, or its `small-thumbnail` when set,
		which is why img/thumbnail.png sits on the first entry.
	-->
	<website>https://github.com/ConductionNL/thematiq</website>
	<bugs>https://github.com/ConductionNL/thematiq/issues</bugs>
	<repository type="git">https://github.com/ConductionNL/thematiq.git</repository>

	<screenshot small-thumbnail="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ConductionNL/thematiq/main/img/thumbnail.png">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ConductionNL/thematiq/main/img/screenshot-1-compare.png</screenshot>
	<screenshot>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ConductionNL/thematiq/main/img/screenshot-2-editor-primary-button.png</screenshot>
	<screenshot>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ConductionNL/thematiq/main/img/screenshot-3-editor-buttons.png</screenshot>

	<dependencies>
		<php min-version="8.3"/>
		<!-- Floor 32, aligned with the fleet. Two reasons, both verifiable here:

		     1. PHP. This app already requires 8.3. Nextcloud 31 and below run on
		        8.1/8.2 as well, so a 28 floor advertised a range where the app
		        store could offer this app to an instance whose PHP it cannot run
		        on. NC32 is the first release whose own floor is 8.3, so 32 is
		        where the two declarations stop contradicting each other.

		     2. It is what is actually tested. `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml`
		        pins `nextcloud-test-refs: '["stable34", "stable32"]'`, so both
		        ends of this range carry PHPUnit legs: `NC stable32` proves the
		        floor is real, `NC stable34` proves the ceiling is. Nothing has
		        exercised 28-31 since #240. Declaring support for versions no leg
		        touches is a promise the app store passes on to users, which is
		        why tests/Unit/ClaimAccuracyTest asserts that the lowest ref in
		        that matrix EQUALS the min-version on the line below.

		     History, because this attribute has moved twice: #241 raised it to 32
		     and #242 restored 28, on the stated premise that "this repo does not
		     set nextcloud-test-refs, so it inherits the shared default of
		     [stable31, stable32]". That premise was already false when #242
		     landed — #240 had pinned the list to stable32 one commit earlier —
		     and it is false now: development's own run has only `NC stable32`
		     legs. #242's other premise (openregister restoring its 28 floor) does
		     not bear on this app at all, which declares no <app> dependency.

		     max-version stays 34, which is the fleet-wide value; openconnector is
		     the only repo that says 35. -->
		<nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="35"/>
	</dependencies>

	<!-- Upstream token freshness check (openspec/specs/upstream-freshness/spec.md):
	     daily, time-insensitive, disabled by default (admin opt-in toggle). -->
	<background-jobs>
		<job>OCA\Thematiq\BackgroundJob\UpstreamFreshnessJob</job>
		<job>OCA\Thematiq\BackgroundJob\ScheduledSwitchJob</job>
		<job>OCA\Thematiq\BackgroundJob\ConfigSourceJob</job>
	</background-jobs>

	<!--
		ORDERING IN THIS BLOCK IS LOAD-BEARING.

		The three Migrate* steps rescue the configuration the app-id rename
		`nldesign` -> `thematiq` would otherwise strand, and they MUST run
		FIRST in each block. Nextcloud namespaces `oc_appconfig` and
		`oc_preferences` by app id and there is no in-place app-id upgrade:
		after the rename every stored row still says `nldesign`, every reader
		asks for `thematiq`, and every reader supplies a default — so NOTHING
		ERRORS. For a theming app that means every token set, override, custom
		font, per-app/per-group exclusion and email-footer field an admin ever
		configured silently reverts to stock, with nothing in the log.

		THE ORDER IS A STANDING INVARIANT, NOT A FACT ABOUT TODAY'S STEP LIST.
		MigrateAppConfigKeys copies a key only when `thematiq` does not already
		hold a value for it, so ANY step that writes configuration under the new
		app id before it runs would make it skip that key as "already present"
		and strand the admin's real value under `nldesign` forever. New repair
		steps therefore go BELOW the three Migrate* steps unless there is a
		specific reason not to.

		As measured on 2026-08-24, GenerateDarkVariantsRepairStep does not in
		fact write any configuration — it is constructed with only
		DarkPaletteService and LoggerInterface, and neither touches IAppConfig;
		it writes css/tokens/dark/{id}.css files. (An earlier version of this
		comment claimed it wrote a `dark_variants_generated` key. No such key
		exists anywhere in the app. The ordering is still correct and still
		required — the invariant above does not depend on that claim — but the
		reason given for it was not true.) The `dark_variants` app-config key is
		written only by SettingsController, from an admin action, never by a
		repair step.

		The steps are registered under <install> as well as <post-migration>
		because <install> is the ONLY hook a renamed app's first enable fires —
		to Nextcloud `thematiq` is a brand-new app, not an upgrade of
		`nldesign`, so <post-migration> alone would never run on the very
		instances that need the migration most.
	-->
	<!--
		Child order is fixed by info.xsd's <xs:sequence>: pre-migration,
		post-migration, live-migration, install, uninstall. It is a sequence and
		not a choice, so <install> before <post-migration> fails validation with
		"Element 'post-migration': This element is not expected. Expected is
		( uninstall )" — the parser is past install and the only thing that may
		still follow is uninstall. Both blocks stay; only the order changes.
	-->
	<repair-steps>
		<post-migration>
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\MigrateStoredClassNames</step>
			<!--
				THE THIRD STORE KEYED BY APP ID, AND THE STEPS ABOVE DO NOT
				COVER IT. `oc_appconfig` and `oc_preferences` are Nextcloud's;
				this one is OpenRegister's
				`openregister_schemas.application`.

				OpenRegister resolves the two halves of a configuration by
				DIFFERENT keys: a REGISTER by slug alone, a SCHEMA by the PAIR
				(application, slug) via findByApplicationAndSlug(). An import
				passes `appId: Application::APP_ID` = `thematiq`, while any
				schema written under the old id still carries
				`application = nldesign` — so the pair matches nothing, and
				ImportHandler's not-found branch is not an error path, it is the
				"create a new one" path. The import then builds a SECOND, EMPTY
				schema set while every stored object stays bound to the old
				rows. Nothing errors; the collections just render empty.

				This app's theming lives in Nextcloud's own IConfig, not in
				OpenRegister objects, so on a clean instance this step finds
				nothing and says "nothing to do". It is registered as the guard
				for an instance that DOES carry `nldesign` schema rows, where
				the failure is silent rather than loud.

				Refuses rather than merges where a slug already has a twin under
				the new application id, never deletes a schema, and never throws
				— an escaping exception in <install> aborts the install.
			-->
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
			<!-- Dark-mode token variants (openspec/specs/dark-mode/spec.md):
			     regenerate missing/stale css/tokens/dark/{id}.css on
			     install/upgrade. Degrades to a logged warning (never fatal)
			     when the app directory is read-only. -->
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\MoveRuntimeFilesToAppData</step>
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Migration\GenerateDarkVariantsRepairStep</step>
			<!-- Typed token values: each overrides file gains the motion twins
			     and its colour overrides' dark scopes on upgrade, values kept.
			     Idempotent, never throws. Not under <install>: a fresh install
			     has no overrides file yet. -->
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\MigrateOverrideValueTypes</step>
			<!-- Theme as code (openspec/specs/theme-as-code/spec.md): apply the
			     branding package named in thematiq.config_source when it
			     changed. Last, after the configuration rescue above. -->
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\ApplyConfigSource</step>
		</post-migration>
		<install>
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\MigrateStoredClassNames</step>
			<!-- Schema half of the same app-id rename; see the note under
			     <post-migration>. An app-id rename presents to Nextcloud as a
			     FRESH INSTALL, so this block is the one that actually fires on
			     the upgrade the step exists for. -->
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
			<!-- Dark-mode token variants — see the note under <post-migration>. -->
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Repair\MoveRuntimeFilesToAppData</step>
			<step>OCA\Thematiq\Migration\GenerateDarkVariantsRepairStep</step>
		</install>
	</repair-steps>
	<commands>
		<command>OCA\Thematiq\Command\ComplianceReport</command>
		<command>OCA\Thematiq\Command\GenerateDarkVariants</command>
		<command>OCA\Thematiq\Command\ConfigExport</command>
		<command>OCA\Thematiq\Command\ConfigImport</command>
		<command>OCA\Thematiq\Command\ConfigApply</command>
		<command>OCA\Thematiq\Command\ConfigVersions</command>
		<command>OCA\Thematiq\Command\ConfigRestore</command>
		<command>OCA\Thematiq\Command\ThemeList</command>
		<command>OCA\Thematiq\Command\ThemeGet</command>
		<command>OCA\Thematiq\Command\ThemeSet</command>
	</commands>
	<settings>
		<admin>OCA\Thematiq\Settings\Admin</admin>
		<admin-section>theming</admin-section>
		<personal>OCA\Thematiq\Settings\Personal</personal>
	</settings>
</info>
